{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And his heart went limp.</b> Do not read it as פוגת (a soothing). The meaning is: his heart rested, for he was no longer startled and did not react to what they told him — <b>for he did not believe</b> — until he saw the wagons. Another interpretation: the language is one of strolling and joy, as in ״אישי כהן גדול״ — ״stand and take one stroll on the floor״ (Yoma) — i.e., the matter was to him like a jest. And the Targum proves it: והוה מלא פייגין על ליביה (and his heart was filled with rejoicing)."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 45, :notes {0 "The citation ״אישי כהן גדול״ is obscure; rendered hyper-literally as it stands. The Yoma reference (Yoma 18b) concerns the High Priest kept awake on Yom Kippur; the precise linkage here is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:08:45.183996116Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}